KINETIC_SUMMARY_PRECISION_TASK = 'Determine what the selected kinetic measurements and their repetition establish about the assayed reference molecule’s activity. Identify the actual organism, molecule, mutations, purification construct, expression host, assay system, conditions, substrate, endpoint, parameter and normalization. Keep purification tags separate from an established assayed tag state. Possible functions may emerge from supplied sequence, contents or snippets; an initial annotation is not required.\n\nIdentify the exact source row, uncertainty label, count and applicable footnote or Methods passage. Establish which construct each statement describes. Distinguish independent preparations from repeated assays, fitted curves, concentrations and repeated representations. Preserve unknown preparation identities and missing raw values.\n\nEstablish what the reported central estimate and spread summarize. Keep physical preparation count separate from the statistical count used by an estimator. Determine whether the SD describes preparation-level estimates and whether its normalization and averaging basis are established. Preserve assumptions as explicit conditional interpretations. Calculating mean precision does not require a normality assumption; a Student-t interval requires additional distributional support.\n\nExamine comparability outside the intended change, controls, background contributors, calibration and the endpoint’s bearing on the functional assertion. Independent preparations can reveal preparation variability while sharing calibration or other systematic errors. A control on a different construct does not supply a matched blank. A reproducible artificial-substrate reaction does not establish a preferred physiological substrate.\n\nSelect source operands for deterministic reading and calculation. Return the requested numerical components with their units, sampling basis, assumptions and unavailable reasons. Preserve useful single-construct results when a comparison is incomplete. SD, count, variance, SE and an interval share an uncertainty contribution; none supplies an assay error probability, target-transfer weight or final annotation credit.\n'

